<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="app-coding-sheet">
  <title>Coding Sheet Data Dictionary</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This appendix defines the information that must be preserved when a response is
      coded. It is authoritative at the level of meaning and controlled values. A CSV
      may place all fields in one wide table, while a database may separate response,
      episode, review, and provenance records. Either representation is acceptable if
      it preserves the same distinctions and stable identifiers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The analytic unit is normally one row per episode. A response with several
      episodes therefore appears on several episode rows. A response with no identifiable
      episode still requires a response-level record for screening, rationale, and
      disposition; an episode-sheet implementation may represent this as a stub row with
      <c>episode_n</c> blank and <c>complete</c> equal to 0.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-app-coding-sheet-authority">
      <title>Use the Assigned Schema</title>
      <p>
        Field names below are the canonical names used in this book. When an assigned
        database or sheet uses different physical column names, follow the supplied
        mapping rather than creating parallel columns. Never rename identifiers or
        normalize student text during coding.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark xml:id="rem-app-coding-sheet-versions">
      <title>Authoritative home and mirrored versions</title>
      <p>
        This appendix is the single authoritative in-book home for field meanings and
        controlled values. It mirrors Coder's Handbook v0.2 §5 and Coding Protocol
        <m>\pi</m> 0.1 §7 (which added <c>link_recorded</c>, <c>goal_coverage</c>,
        <c>alt_codes</c>, and <c>protocol_version</c>); if this appendix and those
        frozen instruments ever disagree, the frozen instruments govern and this
        appendix is the defect. Every other book location that lists fields or values
        — worksheets, the protocol summary, chapter tables — is a cross-referencing
        view of this appendix, not a second specification.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-coding-sheet-fields">
    <title>Episode-Level Fields</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-app-coding-sheet-response-fields">
      <title>Response Identity and Screening</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-app-coding-sheet-response-fields">
        <title>Response-level fields.</title>
        <tabular top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%" halign="center" />
          <col width="72%" halign="left" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Definition and entry rule</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copy exactly. It maps every coder record to the same response.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>question_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copy exactly. It maps to the exact question text and question-specific guidelines.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>screening_decision</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                Records whether the response continues to episode analysis. Screening cares about analyzable responses, not mathematical correctness. Possible values:
                <c>INCLUDE</c>, <c>EXCLUDE</c>, or <c>FLAG</c>.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>screening_rationale</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>States the visible basis for the screening decision. Avoid vague rationales such as <q>unclear</q> without identifying what is missing.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>missing_goals</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Lists parts of question prompt that are not addressed by the response. Keep separate from episode correctness.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>response_status</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                A special label that described the overall condition of the response. Possible values:
              </p>
              <p>
                <line><c>ORDINARY</c>: no special condition found.</line>
                <line><c>OOV</c>: out of the current vocabulary.</line>
                <line><c>NO_MATCH</c>: no code in the codebook fits any episode.</line>
                <line><c>UNRESOLVED</c>: the response needs discussion.</line>
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>disposition</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records the next action. The schema permits six values: <c>auto_accept</c>, <c>standard_review</c>, <c>problem_type_flag</c>, <c>terminology_flag</c>, <c>no_match</c>, or <c>escalate</c>. The current deterministic baseline never produces <c>problem_type_flag</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-app-coding-sheet-episode-fields">
      <title>Episode Content and Structure</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-app-coding-sheet-episode-fields">
        <title>Fields that describe one episode.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%"/>
          <col width="72%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Definition and entry rule</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>episode_n</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Numbers episodes within a response in the order their judgments appear. Leave blank when there is no identifiable episode. Possible values: <c>1</c>, <c>2</c>, <c>3</c>, …</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>criterion_verbatim</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copies the student's words that provide <m>C_k</m>. Do not fill-in any unstated justification gaps here.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>link_recorded</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records the minimal link sufficient to connect the criterion to the judgment or <c>[none stated]</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>judgment_verbatim</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copies the student's words that provide <m>J_k</m>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>link_stated</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Describes link evidence only: an explicit connector, a recoverable unstated justification, or no recoverable justification. Possible values: <c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, or <c>absent</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>complete</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Indicates when both a criterion and a judgment are identifiable. An absent link alone does not make the episode incomplete. Possible values: <c>0</c> or <c>1</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>relation</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records how this episode relates to other episodes in the same response. Possible values: <c>chained(k)</c> when this episode's criterion is episode <em>k</em>'s judgment, <c>independent</c> when it stands apart, or blank for a lone episode (Handbook §5; Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> §6 interim rule). Detected nesting is noted in <c>notes</c> pending the open retention decision; richer values such as <c>nested(k)</c> are proposals, not current sheet values.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-app-coding-sheet-analytic-fields">
      <title>Matching, Correctness, Coverage, and Uncertainty</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-app-coding-sheet-analytic-fields">
        <title>Analytic annotation fields.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="21%"/>
          <col width="22%"/>
          <col width="57%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Allowed form</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Definition and entry rule</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>code</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One accepted code ID or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Primary control-structure match for this episode. Leave blank during a blind pass and when no current code is adequate.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>alt_codes</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Zero or more code IDs</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Preserves other plausible codes for the same episode. Do not use this field for codes belonging to separate episodes.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>oov_note</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Verbatim unfamiliar term or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copies relevant terminology not represented adequately in the controlled vocabulary. Do not silently translate it into preferred course language.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>c1</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>0, 1, or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Criterion correctness: 1 when the criterion is true in the actual problem state, 0 when false. Leave blank for incomplete or unevaluable episodes.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>c2</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>0, 1, or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Inferential validity: 1 when the criterion licenses the judgment for this question, 0 otherwise. Evaluated from the criterion and judgment as written; an absent link never blocks the digits and does not by itself determine the value (Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> §4).</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>goal_coverage</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Goal identifiers, <c>off-target</c>, or <c>unclear</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Lists the question goals addressed by this episode's judgment. It does not alter <c>c1</c> or <c>c2</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>confidence</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>3, 2, or 1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Coder confidence in the recorded analysis: 3 confident, 2 plausible but arguable, 1 genuinely unsure. This is not student correctness.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>notes</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Brief evidence-based text</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records ambiguity, alternatives, policy questions, or context needed later. A confidence value of 1 requires a specific note.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-app-coding-sheet-provenance-fields">
      <title>Provenance and Review History</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-app-coding-sheet-provenance-fields">
        <title>Fields required to interpret and audit the coding record.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="21%"/>
          <col width="22%"/>
          <col width="57%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Allowed form</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Definition and entry rule</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>coder_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Approved project identifier</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Identifies the coder without placing personal information in ordinary analysis exports.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>coded_at</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Approved timestamp format</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records when the independent or assigned pass was completed.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>handbook_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version of the workflow and handbook guidance governing the record.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>protocol_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version of the coding protocol governing policy choices.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>codebook_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version label or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Question-specific codebook used for matching. Leave blank during a genuinely blind pass.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>vocabulary_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version label or blank</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Controlled-vocabulary version available during extraction or review.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>question_frame_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Version label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Identifies the normative question frame used for correctness and coverage decisions.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>review_action</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>confirm</c>, <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>, <c>replace</c>, <c>reject_all</c>, or assigned value</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records a human action on a prior proposal. Store it as a new review record rather than overwriting the proposal.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>review_rationale</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Brief evidence-based text</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Explains the review action and identifies the affected decision.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>disagreement_type</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One or more taxonomy values</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Classifies the earliest point of divergence. Possible values: <c>SCREEN</c>, <c>BOUNDARY</c>, <c>CRITERION</c>, <c>LINK</c>, <c>JUDGMENT</c>, <c>RELATION</c>, <c>MATCH</c>, <c>CORRECTNESS</c>, <c>COVERAGE</c>, <c>VOCABULARY</c>, <c>POLICY</c>, or <c>OTHER</c>; the defining taxonomy is <xref ref="tab-disagreement-taxonomy"/>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>adjudication_status</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>not_required</c>, <c>pending</c>, <c>resolved</c>, or <c>unresolved</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Records whether comparison produced a final decision. An unresolved result is preserved rather than replaced by forced agreement.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        A physical sheet may repeat response- and provenance-level values on every
        episode row. A normalized database may store them once and join them by stable
        identifiers. The research meaning is unchanged.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-coding-sheet-examples">
    <title>Completed Rows</title>

    <p>
      The rows below illustrate the most important combinations. They are abbreviated:
      required version and reviewer fields are represented by the final provenance
      column rather than repeated individually.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-app-coding-sheet-completed-rows">
      <title>Representative coding-sheet rows.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="10%"/>
        <col width="7%"/>
        <col width="17%"/>
        <col width="17%"/>
        <col width="17%"/>
        <col width="9%"/>
        <col width="9%"/>
        <col width="14%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Response</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Ep.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Link</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Judgment</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Status</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Correct.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Code / note</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>EX-01</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>There is a free variable</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Free variable rules out one unique solution</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>the solution is not unique</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>explicit</c>; complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>CS-free-variable</c>; confidence 3</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>EX-02</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>fewer pivots than variables</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p>An unpivoted variable is free</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>one variable is free</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>explicit</c>; complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>CS-pivot-count</c>; relation blank, chain recorded on episode 2</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>EX-02</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>2</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>one variable is free</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Free variable rules out one unique solution</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>the solution is not unique</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>explicit</c>; <c>chained(1)</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>CS-free-variable</c>; confidence 3</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>EX-03</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>—</p></cell>
          <cell><p>—</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>[none stated]</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>No, the solution is not unique.</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p>incomplete stub</p></cell>
          <cell><p>—</p></cell>
          <cell><p>bare answer; no episode code; confidence 3</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p><c>EX-04</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>The solution set has one degree of freedom</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p>One degree of freedom gives a line, not a point</p></cell>
          <cell><p><q>it is a line rather than a point</q></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>explicit</c>; complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>OOV term preserved; no forced code; confidence 2</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The example codes are descriptive placeholders, not replacements for the current
      frozen codebook IDs. In project data, copy the assigned code IDs and version label
      exactly. See <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/> for the policies that
      govern completion of these fields.
    </p>
  </section>
</appendix>